# A plan that cannot work, and the shortest explanation of why.
#
# This model is deliberately impossible. Rather than reporting "no solution",
# Plimsoll deletes assumptions one at a time and keeps only those it cannot do
# without, so what you get back is the irreducible core of the contradiction.
#
#   plimsoll examples/saas-unit-economics.plim    # exits 2
#
# Note which lines are *absent* from the conflict: the payback relations below
# are satisfiable and so play no part in it.

dimension money
unit USD = money
unit customer

given cac           = 900 .. 1_400 [USD/customer]
given monthly_price = 40 .. 60 [USD/month/customer]
given gross_margin  = 72 .. 82 [%]
given monthly_churn = 2.5 .. 4 [%/month]

# A customer's expected lifetime is the reciprocal of the churn rate, which is
# only dimensionally sensible because churn is a rate: 1/(1/month) is a month.
lifetime = 1 / monthly_churn

ltv           = monthly_price * gross_margin * lifetime
ltv_cac_ratio = ltv / cac

payback = cac / (monthly_price * gross_margin)

# Satisfiable, and therefore not part of the conflict.
payback <= 60 [month]

# Not satisfiable. On these assumptions the ratio cannot exceed about 2.2.
ltv_cac_ratio >= 3
